Yes, I think I found the slowdown and a fix, using a standard trick from integer programming. I tested the idea in Python but not in Mathematica.

**Why it's slow.** Each case is linear equations in integer unknowns: the 27 class counts c, the 26 lattice coefficients z, and the fixed mod-9 sums. The solver first relaxes to real numbers, and over the reals the lattice condition says nothing, because any real vector is some real combination of the basis. So the relaxation looks feasible everywhere and the solver branches blindly. I imitated a weak solver with a deliberately naive branch-and-bound. On this formulation it gave up after 3,000 nodes on each case I tried, without a verdict.

**The fix.** This is a reformulation from Aardal, Hurkens and Lenstra (2000) for integer programs with equality constraints. Write every integer solution of the equations as x₀ + y·K, where K is an LLL-reduced basis of the integer solutions of the homogeneous system. Here that basis has 18 vectors. The only constraints left are 0 ≤ c ≤ class size, and the solver branches on the 18 coefficients y. Because the basis is reduced, each branch cuts the box along a direction where it is thin, so infeasibility shows up after a few branches. With the same naive branch-and-bound:
- all 2025 cases came back infeasible;
- each needed about 9 branch nodes on average, 67 at most, about 58 s in total;
- the n = 528 control came back feasible after 5 nodes.

**Mathematica version, untested:**

```wolfram
ClearAll[m, size, latticeBasis, B27, cand, T0, T1, d9s, Amat, uu, hh, rk, Kred, Kc, particular, lastStepAHL, yy];
m = 10;
size[n_, M_, r_] := Length[Range[r, n - 1, M]];
latticeBasis[M_] := Module[{e = Table[Binomial[m, t] (-1)^(m - t), {t, 0, m}], g},
   g = Table[Module[{v = ConstantArray[0, M]},
      Do[v[[Mod[t + k, M] + 1]] += e[[t + 1]], {t, 0, m}]; v], {k, 0, M - 1}];
   DeleteCases[Last[HermiteDecomposition[g]], {0 ..}]];
B27 = latticeBasis[27];
cand = Append[#, 81 - Total[#]] & /@ Select[Tuples[Range[-57, 57, 6], 2], Abs[81 - Total[#]] <= 57 &];
T0 = Select[cand, Mod[#[[1]] - #[[2]], 9] == 3 &];
T1 = Select[cand, Mod[#[[1]] - #[[2]], 9] == 6 &];
d9s = Flatten[Table[{a[[1]], b[[1]], -57, a[[2]], b[[2]], -57, a[[3]], b[[3]], -48}, {a, T0}, {b, T1}], 1];

(* unknowns x = (c_0..c_26, z_1..z_26): 2 c_r - Sum_k z_k B27[[k, r+1]] == size_r, plus the 9 mod-9 class sums of c *)
Amat = Join[
   Table[Join[2 UnitVector[27, r + 1], -B27[[All, r + 1]]], {r, 0, 26}],
   Table[Join[Table[Boole[Mod[j, 9] == q], {j, 0, 26}], ConstantArray[0, Length[B27]]], {q, 0, 8}]];
{uu, hh} = HermiteDecomposition[Transpose[Amat]];      (* uu . Transpose[Amat] == hh *)
rk = MatrixRank[Amat];
Kred = LatticeReduce[uu[[rk + 1 ;;]]];                   (* reduced basis of the integer solutions of Amat.x == 0 *)
Kc = Kred[[All, 1 ;; 27]];
particular[b_] := Module[{w = Quiet@LinearSolve[Transpose[hh[[1 ;; rk]]], b]},
   If[VectorQ[w, IntegerQ], Transpose[uu[[1 ;; rk]]] . w, $Failed]];
SetAttributes[yy, NHoldAll];
lastStepAHL[n_, d9_] := Module[{sz = Table[size[n, 27, r], {r, 0, 26}], x0, y, c},
   x0 = particular[Join[sz, Table[(d9[[q + 1]] + size[n, 9, q])/2, {q, 0, 8}]]];
   If[x0 === $Failed, False,
    y = Array[yy, Length[Kred]];
    c = x0[[1 ;; 27]] + y . Kc;
    VectorQ[Quiet[LinearOptimization[0, N[Join[Thread[0 <= c], Thread[c <= sz]]],
        Map[Element[#, Integers] &, y], "PrimalMinimizer"]], NumberQ]]];

Print["kernel dimension (expect 18): ", Length[Kred], "   kernel check (expect True): ", AllTrue[Kred, Amat . # == ConstantArray[0, 36] &]];
Print["control, n=528 (expect True): ", lastStepAHL[528, {21, 39, 21, 3, -33, -51, -24, -6, 30}]];
Print["feasible cases among the 2025 at n=512 (expect {}): ", AbsoluteTiming[Select[d9s, lastStepAHL[512, #] &]]];
```

